# Shops and shopkeepers
Never buy from a shop that has no change|The change is your name|A man buys a packet of tea from a shop with no change; the shopkeeper writes his name in a ledger and says the change will come later.|MAN; SHOPKEEPER; WIFE|A corner shop with an old ledger|He didn't have exact change|The ledger is a list of people who owe the shop their names|The ledger, the man's name, the shopkeeper's pen|debt|eerie
Don't ask a shopkeeper to keep your bag|He keeps it forever|A woman asks a shopkeeper to hold her bag while she runs an errand; she returns and the shop is closed, with her bag still inside.|WOMAN; SHOPKEEPER; HUSBAND|A shop with a metal shutter|She was in a hurry|The shopkeeper died years ago and the shop opens only for him|The shutter, the bag, the woman's hand|invitation|eerie
Never buy the last item on a shelf|It was waiting for someone|A boy buys the last toy on a shelf; the toy starts crying at night, and the shopkeeper says it was waiting for its previous owner.|BOY; MOTHER; SHOPKEEPER|A toy shop with one shelf|He wanted it|The previous owner is the shopkeeper's dead son|The toy, the empty shelf, the boy's bed|invitation|sad
Don't take a receipt with no total|The total is your time|A man gets a receipt with no total; every day the total increases by one hour of his life.|MAN; CASHIER; WIFE|A supermarket checkout|He didn't check|The receipt is a bill for the time he has left|The receipt, the man's watch, the checkout|counting|eerie
Never haggle with a shopkeeper who doesn't blink|He takes the difference from your life|A woman haggles with a shopkeeper who never blinks; she gets a discount, and the next day she can't remember her daughter's name.|WOMAN; SHOPKEEPER; DAUGHTER|A fabric shop with bolts of cloth|She wanted a better price|The shopkeeper is collecting memories as payment|The cloth, the woman's blank face, the shopkeeper's eyes|debt|eerie
Don't enter a shop that has no door|You leave through a different one|A traveler enters a shop with no door; he leaves through a door that opens into his childhood home.|TRAVELER; SHOPKEEPER; MOTHER|A shop with a blank wall|He needed directions|The shop is a way station between lives|The blank wall, the old house, the traveler's bag|threshold|sad
Never buy from a mirror-only shop|You see all the customers|A woman buys a mirror from a mirror-only shop; the mirror shows every customer who ever bought one, and they are all looking at her.|WOMAN; SHOPKEEPER; HUSBAND|A shop lined with mirrors|She needed a mirror|The customers are trapped in the mirrors|The mirror, the crowd, the woman's reflection|reflection|eerie
Don't ask for credit|The shop remembers|A man asks a shopkeeper for credit; the shopkeeper agrees, and every night the man hears the shop's shutter open in his bedroom.|MAN; SHOPKEEPER; WIFE|A grocery shop with a shutter|He was short on cash|The shop is collecting the debt from his sleep|The shutter, the man's bed, the ledger|debt|eerie
