Never ignore the expiry date|The medicine expires you|A man takes expired medicine; his body starts aging faster, and the expiry date appears on his skin.|MAN; WIFE; DOCTOR|A bathroom with a medicine box|He didn't check|The medicine is counting his remaining time|The box, the man's old face, the mirror|counting|eerie
Don't ask a pharmacist for a recommendation|He recommends your own prescription|A woman asks a pharmacist for a recommendation; he gives her the prescription she will need in ten years.|WOMAN; PHARMACIST; HUSBAND|A pharmacy counter|She trusted him|The prescription is for her husband's death|The prescription, the woman's hand, the pharmacist's smile|witness|eerie
Never buy the last box of medicine|Someone needs it more|A man buys the last box of medicine; a woman arrives, and the pharmacist says the man has taken her son's medicine.|MAN; WOMAN; PHARMACIST|A pharmacy shelf|He was in a hurry|The woman's son is the man's future self|The box, the woman's face, the empty shelf|debt|eerie
Don't return medicine|It returns|A woman returns medicine to a pharmacy; the medicine returns to her house, on her table, every night.|WOMAN; PHARMACIST; HUSBAND|A pharmacy counter|It was the wrong dose|The medicine is the pharmacist's dead wife's|The bottle, the woman's table, the pharmacist's hand|contamination|eerie
Never take medicine prescribed to someone else|You get their illness|A man takes his friend's medicine; he starts showing his friend's symptoms, and his friend recovers.|MAN; FRIEND; DOCTOR|A kitchen with a medicine box|He had the same headache|The medicine transfers the illness|The box, the man's hands, the friend's smile|contamination|eerie
Don't trust a pharmacy that smells of flowers|The flowers are from a funeral|A woman enters a pharmacy that smells of flowers; the pharmacist says the flowers are from a funeral, and the funeral is hers.|WOMAN; PHARMACIST; HUSBAND|A pharmacy with flowers|She needed a prescription|The pharmacy is preparing her funeral|The flowers, the woman's prescription, the pharmacist's smile|departure|eerie
# Repair shops
Never repair an object that belonged to a dead person|It repairs them|A man repairs his dead father's radio; the radio starts playing his father's voice, and his father starts repairing the man's life.|MAN; WIFE; FATHER|A repair shop with a radio|He wanted to hear him|The repair is bringing the father back|The radio, the man's house, the father's tools|mimicry|sad
Don't ask a repairman to fix a mirror|He fixes what's in it|A woman asks a repairman to fix a cracked mirror; he fixes the crack, and the reflection in the mirror changes.|WOMAN; REPAIRMAN; HUSBAND|A repair shop with a mirror|It was a family heirloom|The mirror is now showing the repairman's room|The mirror, the repairman's reflection, the woman's hand|reflection|eerie
Never leave a broken watch for repair|It repairs your time|A man leaves a broken watch for repair; the watch is fixed, but the man loses a day every time he checks it.|MAN; REPAIRMAN; WIFE|A watch repair shop|It was his father's|The watch is stealing his days|The watch, the man's calendar, the repairman's tools|counting|eerie
Don't fix a radio that only plays one voice|The voice fixes you|A man fixes a radio that only plays one voice; the voice starts giving him instructions, and he follows them.|MAN; WIFE; THE VOICE|A repair shop with a radio|He wanted music|The voice is the radio's dead owner|The radio, the man's hands, the wife's face|mimicry|eerie
Never repair a door that won't stay closed|It opens somewhere else|A carpenter repairs a door that won't stay closed; the door now opens into a different house every time.|CARPENTER; HOMEOWNER; WIFE|A house with a stubborn door|It was letting in dust|The door is a passage|The door, the other house, the carpenter's tools|threshold|eerie
Don't take a repaired object before paying|It takes something of yours|A customer takes his repaired radio before paying; the radio takes his voice as payment.|CUSTOMER; REPAIRMAN; WIFE|A repair shop counter|He was in a hurry|The radio is collecting payment in kind|The radio, the customer's silent mouth, the repairman|debt|eerie
Never ask a repairman what was wrong with it|He tells you, and it becomes true|A woman asks a repairman what was wrong with her clock; he says it was counting her time, and the clock starts counting again.|WOMAN; REPAIRMAN; HUSBAND|A repair shop with a clock|She was curious|The clock was stopped because it reached her death|The clock, the woman's face, the repairman's smile|counting|eerie
Don't repair a child's toy|The child comes back to play|A father repairs his dead daughter's toy; the toy starts playing by itself, and the daughter's voice asks him to join her.|FATHER; MOTHER; DAUGHTER'S VOICE|A child's bedroom with a toy|He couldn't throw it away|The daughter wants him to play|The toy, the father's hand, the empty room|invitation|sad
Never use a repair shop that has no customers|The repairman has been waiting|A man enters a repair shop with no customers; the repairman says he has been waiting for someone with the man's broken object.|MAN; REPAIRMAN; WIFE|A repair shop with dust|His watch was broken|The repairman is the man's future self|The shop, the watch, the repairman's face|mimicry|eerie
Don't leave your name at a repair shop|They call you when it's ready|A woman leaves her name and phone number at a repair shop; the shop calls her every night, but no one speaks.|WOMAN; REPAIRMAN; HUSBAND|A repair shop counter|She wanted to be notified|The calls are from the object, not the shop|The phone, the woman's name, the repairman's tools|mimicry|eerie
